<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Baker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Samuel White Baker</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Baker was an English explorer who in 1864, with his partner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-albert">Lake Albert</a> and saw the waterfall he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murchison-falls">Murchison Falls</a>. From 1869 to 1873 he served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail</a> as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equatoria">Equatoria</a>, trying to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and extend Egyptian rule south. In 1872 he proclaimed the annexation of Bunyoro at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masindi">Masindi</a> but was driven out by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabalega">Kabalega</a>'s forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-baker.jpg" alt="Samuel Baker" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LeonisRugitur · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir-Baker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
